# Drug-specific medical paragraphs (onset x2, peak x2, comedown x2) replacing shared category text.
from gen import D
X={
"cannabis-flower":("Inhaled THC crosses from the lungs into blood within seconds, and blood levels peak within about 10 minutes, before the high itself peaks, because THC must move from blood into the brain.","Heart rate can rise sharply in the first minutes and blood pressure may drop on standing, which is why some first-time users faint.","At the peak, CB1 activation in the hippocampus and cortex disrupts short-term memory and attention; activation in the cerebellum and basal ganglia slows reaction time, which is why driving is impaired.","Anxiety and paranoia at the peak are linked to THC acting on the amygdala; CBD in the flower may soften this, while high-THC, low-CBD strains make it more likely.","As THC moves from blood into fat, effects fade within a few hours even though THC remains in the body for days or weeks in regular users.","In daily users, falling levels may bring irritability and poor sleep; tolerance comes from CB1 receptors reducing in number and partly recovers after about four weeks off."),
"cannabis-concentrates":("Concentrates deliver a large amount of THC through the lungs at once, so blood levels spike higher and faster than with flower.","The heart-rate jump is larger, and dizziness or fainting is more common, especially when standing up.","The peak is dominated by very high CB1 activation with little CBD to balance it; studies link daily high-potency use to roughly several-fold higher odds of psychosis.","Very high exposure is associated with cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome in heavy users and with acute panic and vomiting in occasional users.","Because the dose is concentrated, the fall from peak is steeper and fatigue can be heavy.","Tolerance builds especially fast with daily dabbing, and withdrawal (irritability, insomnia, low appetite, vivid dreams) is more marked."),
"cannabis-edibles":("Swallowed THC is absorbed slowly and unevenly from the gut, and only a fraction survives first-pass liver metabolism; much is converted to 11-hydroxy-THC, which crosses into the brain readily.","Food in the stomach can delay onset by an hour or more, which is the main reason people take extra before the first amount works.","At the peak, 11-hydroxy-THC levels can be high relative to THC, giving a heavier, more sedating and more disorienting effect than smoked cannabis.","Emergency departments see edible cases with vomiting, fast heart rate, panic and, in children, deep sedation, low breathing effort and seizures.","Effects persist for many hours as the gut keeps absorbing THC and the liver keeps producing 11-hydroxy-THC.","Next-day impairment is common; children may need hospital observation until fully awake."),
"delta-8-thc":("Delta-8-THC is a partial CB1 agonist with roughly half to two-thirds the potency of delta-9-THC in older studies.","Body signs mirror THC: dry mouth, red eyes and a faster heartbeat.","Peak effects include sedation and time distortion; poison center reports show confusion, vomiting and loss of consciousness, especially in children.","Contaminants from the conversion process, not just delta-8 itself, may contribute to harm.","Effects fade over a few hours (vapes) or many hours (edibles).","Delta-8 shows up on standard cannabis drug tests."),
"thc-o":("THC-O itself is thought to be inactive until liver and blood enzymes remove the acetate group to release delta-8 or delta-9 THC, which explains the delay.","The delay leads people to take more before effects begin.","At the peak, effects are those of the released THC, which can be strong because acetylated products are often concentrated.","When vaped, acetate compounds can break down into ketene, a gas toxic to the lungs.","Effects last longer than ordinary THC because conversion continues over time.","Lung symptoms (cough, chest pain, shortness of breath) after vaping need medical attention."),
"hhc":("HHC is made by adding hydrogen to THC-like molecules; one form (9R) binds CB1 strongly, the other (9S) weakly.","Body effects match THC: faster heart rate, dry mouth and red eyes.","Peak effects depend on the 9R-to-9S ratio, which varies by batch.","Newer variants like HHC-P are far more potent and have caused emergency visits in Europe.","Effects fade like THC.","HHC metabolites may or may not appear on standard drug tests, but many tests detect them."),
"synthetic-cannabinoids":("Synthetic cannabinoids are full CB1 agonists, many 10 to 100 times more potent than THC at the receptor, and reach the brain within seconds when smoked.","Early signs include racing heart, chest pain, high blood pressure, sweating and vomiting.","At the peak, seizures, severe agitation, psychosis, catatonia and unresponsiveness occur; some compounds also affect heart rhythm and kidneys.","Some batches have been contaminated with brodifacoum, a long-acting rat poison causing severe bleeding that needs weeks of vitamin K treatment.","Effects are short, but active metabolites can prolong toxicity.","Daily users develop severe withdrawal (vomiting, sweating, tremor, insomnia, cravings), sometimes needing hospital care."),
"dronabinol-nabilone":("Dronabinol capsules are absorbed slowly, with only about 10 to 20 percent reaching the bloodstream after first-pass metabolism.","Older adults are more sensitive to dizziness, low blood pressure and confusion.","At peak, patients may feel 'high', anxious or sleepy, which is the main reason doses are adjusted.","Nabilone is longer-acting, with active metabolites lasting much longer than THC.","Effects fade over several hours.","Labels warn against driving until patients know how they respond."),
"ketamine":("Snorted ketamine is absorbed through the nasal lining; intravenous ketamine reaches the brain within seconds, which is why anesthesia uses it.","Ketamine raises heart rate and blood pressure by releasing noradrenaline, unlike most anesthetics, and causes nystagmus and increased saliva.","At the peak, NMDA blockade in the thalamus and cortex disconnects sensory input from awareness; protective airway reflexes are partly preserved, but vomiting while unresponsive can still cause choking.","Emergence reactions (vivid dreams, confusion, agitation) are known from anesthesia, and similar experiences occur in recreational use.","Ketamine's half-life is about 2 to 3 hours; norketamine is active and extends after-effects.","Chronic use damages the bladder lining (ketamine cystitis), causing pain, urgency and blood in urine; stopping early can allow recovery, but severe cases need surgery. Liver and bile duct injury is also reported."),
"esketamine":("Esketamine is absorbed through the nasal lining, with peak levels within about 20 to 40 minutes.","Blood pressure rises in many patients, peaking around 40 minutes, so it is checked before and after.","At peak, dissociation, dizziness and nausea are common; sedation is monitored.","Antidepressant effects are thought to come from glutamate surges and new synaptic connections after the drug wears off.","Most acute effects resolve within two hours.","Bladder symptoms and misuse potential are monitored under the REMS program."),
"pcp":("PCP is fat-soluble and long-lasting, with a half-life that can be about 1 to 2 days, and it is stored in fat and recirculated.","It raises blood pressure, heart rate and temperature and causes characteristic jerky eye movements in several directions.","At the peak, NMDA blockade plus effects on dopamine and sigma receptors produce agitation, psychosis, catatonia, and insensitivity to pain.","Severe toxicity includes very high temperature, muscle breakdown, kidney failure, seizures and coma.","Because PCP lingers and recirculates, symptoms can fluctuate for days.","Repeated use causes memory problems, depression and speech difficulty that can last months."),
"dxm":("DXM is absorbed from the gut and converted by CYP2D6 into dextrorphan, the main dissociative compound.","Poor CYP2D6 metabolizers (about 5 to 10 percent of white people) have much higher DXM levels and longer effects.","At peak, NMDA blockade by dextrorphan causes dissociation, while DXM's serotonin effects add risk of serotonin syndrome with antidepressants.","Coricidin products add chlorpheniramine, an antihistamine that causes delirium, seizures and heart toxicity; acetaminophen-containing products can cause liver failure even days later.","DXM's long half-life means effects linger many hours.","Chronic heavy use has been linked to psychosis and, with combination products, liver damage."),
"nitrous-oxide":("Nitrous oxide is poorly soluble in blood, so it reaches equilibrium in the brain within seconds and leaves just as fast.","Breathing it without oxygen lowers blood oxygen; large tanks deliver very cold gas that can freeze tissue.","At the peak, NMDA blockade and endorphin release give dissociation and pain relief; falls and fainting are the main acute risks.","Using bags, masks, or enclosed spaces has caused deaths by asphyxiation.","Effects end within minutes as the gas is breathed out.","Nitrous oxide irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt in vitamin B12, blocking the enzyme methionine synthase; repeated use causes spinal cord degeneration (myeloneuropathy), numbness and weakness, which needs B12 treatment and may not fully recover."),
"propofol":("Propofol is extremely fat-soluble, crossing into the brain within one arm-to-brain circulation time (about 30 seconds).","It causes pain on injection and quickly lowers blood pressure by relaxing blood vessels.","At the peak, propofol suppresses breathing and airway reflexes; apnea is common at induction, which is why airway equipment and trained staff are required.","Long infusions can cause propofol infusion syndrome (acid build-up, heart failure, muscle breakdown).","Rapid redistribution to fat ends its effect within minutes.","Healthcare worker misuse often ends in death because people use it alone without airway support."),
"etomidate":("Etomidate reaches the brain within a minute; it keeps blood pressure stable, unlike propofol.","Involuntary muscle jerks are common at induction.","At the peak, sedation is deep and breathing can stop.","Etomidate blocks 11-beta-hydroxylase, the adrenal enzyme that makes cortisol; repeated exposure from vapes has caused adrenal insufficiency with weakness, low blood pressure and low sodium.","Its effect ends within minutes after a single dose.","Hong Kong and mainland China report psychiatric symptoms and dependence in young people who vape etomidate."),
"methoxetamine":("MXE was designed as a ketamine analog with a longer half-life and slower onset.","Raised blood pressure and fast heart rate were common in hospital reports.","At peak, deep dissociation, agitation, cerebellar signs (staggering, poor coordination) and catatonia were reported.","Deaths usually involved other drugs.","MXE effects last several hours, longer than ketamine.","Bladder toxicity was demonstrated in animal studies, contrary to its marketing."),
"rc-dissociatives":("Each analog has a different potency and duration; 3-MeO-PCP is active at much smaller amounts than ketamine.","Hypertension, fast heart rate and nystagmus are common.","Peak effects range from ketamine-like dissociation to PCP-like agitation and psychosis.","3-MeO-PCP has been linked to several deaths and severe agitation; 2-FDCK closely mirrors ketamine.","Long-acting analogs prolong confusion for many hours.","Bladder and psychiatric harms are presumed similar to ketamine and PCP."),
"poppers":("Alkyl nitrites are absorbed through the lungs within seconds and release nitric oxide, which relaxes smooth muscle in blood vessels.","Blood pressure falls and heart rate rises reflexively; flushing and headache follow.","At peak, low blood pressure can cause fainting; combined with PDE5 inhibitors (Viagra, Cialis) it can cause collapse, heart attack or stroke.","Nitrites oxidize hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen; swallowing poppers can be fatal and needs methylene blue treatment.","Effects end within minutes.","Repeated use is linked to poppers maculopathy (central vision loss), especially with isopropyl nitrite."),
"solvents":("Solvents are highly fat-soluble and enter the brain within seconds, acting on GABA-A, NMDA and glycine receptors like anesthetics.","Early signs include dizziness, slurred speech and a chemical odor on the breath.","At peak, solvents sensitize the heart muscle to adrenaline, so a surge from fright or exertion can trigger ventricular fibrillation (sudden sniffing death).","Hypoxia from bags, choking on vomit, and burns from flammable vapors are other major causes of death.","Effects fade within minutes to an hour as vapors are exhaled.","Chronic toluene exposure causes white matter damage (toluene leukoencephalopathy), hearing loss, kidney acid-base problems, and liver injury; leaded gasoline adds lead poisoning."),
"aerosols":("Propellants such as difluoroethane enter the brain within seconds.","Ear ringing and dizziness appear almost immediately.","At peak, fluorocarbons sensitize the heart to adrenaline, causing sudden fatal arrhythmias.","The rapid expansion of gas can freeze the airway, causing swelling and choking.","Effects end within minutes.","Repeated use damages the heart, liver and kidneys."),
"amphetamine":("Amphetamine is well absorbed from the gut; dextroamphetamine is the more potent form centrally, and levoamphetamine adds more peripheral effects.","It reverses dopamine and noradrenaline transporters, pushing these chemicals out of neurons, which raises heart rate and blood pressure early.","At peak, dopamine in the striatum and prefrontal cortex is high; at therapeutic levels this improves focus, but at high levels it causes euphoria, stereotyped behavior and psychosis.","Cardiovascular risks include high blood pressure, arrhythmias and, rarely, stroke; labels carry a boxed warning for misuse and dependence.","Half-life is about 10 to 12 hours, longer if urine is alkaline.","Insomnia and appetite loss persist; heavy misuse causes depression during withdrawal."),
"lisdexamfetamine":("Lisdexamfetamine is absorbed intact and cleaved in red blood cells to release dextroamphetamine, so the rate is limited by enzyme activity.","This makes onset slower and peak levels lower than the same amphetamine in IR form.","At peak, effects are those of dextroamphetamine: alertness, raised heart rate and blood pressure.","The FDA label includes a boxed warning for misuse and dependence.","Dextroamphetamine's half-life extends effects into the evening.","Insomnia and appetite loss are the most common lingering effects."),
"methylphenidate":("Methylphenidate blocks dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake rather than releasing them, so its effect depends on ongoing nerve activity.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise modestly.","At peak, high doses can cause anxiety, tremor and psychosis; injected tablets cause talc granulomas in the lungs and eyes.","With alcohol, the liver forms ethylphenidate, raising levels and heart effects.","Short half-life (about 2 to 3 hours) explains the IR rebound.","Long-acting forms reduce rebound but can cause insomnia."),
"methamphetamine":("Methamphetamine crosses into the brain more readily than amphetamine and releases dopamine powerfully while also blocking its breakdown.","Early signs include high blood pressure, fast heart rate, high temperature and dilated pupils.","At peak, very high dopamine causes euphoria and, in heavy use, paranoia and hallucinations; severe toxicity causes hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, stroke and heart attack.","Methamphetamine-related heart failure (cardiomyopathy) and pulmonary hypertension are increasingly common in long-term users.","Half-life of about 10 to 12 hours keeps effects going long after the high.","Chronic use reduces dopamine transporters in the brain; partial recovery takes months to years of abstinence."),
"cocaine":("Snorted cocaine also numbs the nasal lining and narrows its blood vessels, slowing its own absorption slightly.","It blocks dopamine, noradrenaline and serotonin reuptake and also blocks sodium channels, which affects heart rhythm.","At peak, coronary arteries narrow, heart oxygen demand rises and platelets clump, so heart attack can occur even in young people; stroke and aortic dissection are also linked.","With alcohol, the liver forms cocaethylene, which lasts longer and is more toxic to the heart.","Cocaine's half-life is under an hour, which drives repeated use.","Chronic snorting can perforate the nasal septum; levamisole adulteration causes skin necrosis and low white cell counts."),
"crack-cocaine":("Smoked cocaine reaches the brain in seconds, faster than snorting, producing a sharp dopamine spike.","Blood pressure and heart rate surge immediately.","At peak, the same heart and stroke risks as cocaine apply, plus lung injury (crack lung) from hot smoke.","Binge use can cause seizures and severe agitation.","The rapid fall in dopamine drives intense craving within minutes.","Chronic use damages lungs, causes burns to lips and fingers, and spreads hepatitis C through shared pipes."),
"modafinil":("Modafinil is absorbed slowly, peaking in 2 to 4 hours, and has a half-life of about 15 hours.","It weakly blocks dopamine reuptake and increases histamine, orexin and noradrenaline activity.","At peak, effects are wakefulness and modest focus; high amounts cause anxiety, tachycardia and insomnia.","Rare but serious rashes (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, DRESS) need immediate medical care.","Its long half-life means sleep may be disrupted the following night.","It speeds up the breakdown of hormonal contraceptives for up to a month after stopping."),
"caffeine":("Caffeine is almost completely absorbed within 45 minutes.","It blocks adenosine A1 and A2A receptors, raising alertness, heart rate and urine production.","At high levels, it causes tremor, palpitations, vomiting and arrhythmias; severe poisoning lowers potassium and causes seizures.","Concentrated powders and liquids have caused deaths because the line between a normal and a lethal amount is invisible.","Half-life is about 5 hours, longer in pregnancy and with some medicines.","Daily users get withdrawal headaches, fatigue and low mood within a day of stopping."),
"nicotine":("Inhaled nicotine reaches the brain within about 10 to 20 seconds.","It activates nicotinic receptors, releasing dopamine, adrenaline and other chemicals, raising heart rate and blood pressure.","At high exposure, nicotine causes nausea, vomiting, sweating and, in poisoning, seizures and breathing muscle paralysis.","Children who swallow e-liquid or pouches are at greatest risk.","Half-life is about 2 hours, which drives frequent use.","Dependence is strong; withdrawal brings irritability, anxiety, poor concentration and craving."),
"phentermine":("Phentermine is absorbed well and releases noradrenaline more than dopamine.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At high levels, agitation, psychosis and arrhythmias can occur.","Combined with serotonergic drugs (as with fenfluramine in fen-phen), heart valve and lung artery damage occurred.","Half-life is about 20 hours, explaining day-long effects.","Insomnia and dry mouth are common."),
"diethylpropion":("Diethylpropion is absorbed quickly and converted to active metabolites.","It releases noradrenaline, raising heart rate modestly.","At high levels, it causes agitation and can trigger seizures in susceptible people.","Long-term use is rarely linked to pulmonary hypertension.","Effects fade over several hours.","Insomnia and restlessness may persist."),
"phendimetrazine":("Phendimetrazine is converted to phenmetrazine, which releases dopamine and noradrenaline.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At high levels, euphoria, agitation and psychosis can occur.","Phenmetrazine itself was withdrawn because of misuse.","Effects fade over several hours.","Insomnia is common."),
"ephedrine":("Ephedrine acts both directly on adrenergic receptors and by releasing noradrenaline.","Blood pressure and heart rate rise quickly.","At high levels, especially with caffeine, ephedrine can cause stroke, heart attack, arrhythmia and seizures; these led to the 2004 ephedra ban.","Pseudoephedrine has weaker effects but can still raise blood pressure.","Half-life is about 6 hours.","Insomnia and urinary retention are common."),
"khat-cathinone":("Cathinone is absorbed through the mouth lining during chewing.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At peak, mild euphoria and talkativeness; heavy use can trigger psychosis.","Long-term use is linked to heart attack, liver injury and oral cancers.","Cathinone is quickly converted to cathine and norephedrine, which are weaker.","Insomnia and low mood follow long sessions."),
"mephedrone":("Mephedrone is absorbed fast and releases both serotonin and dopamine, like a mix of MDMA and cocaine.","Vasoconstriction causes cold or bluish fingers; heart rate rises.","At peak, overheating, agitation and arrhythmias are the main dangers.","Rapid redosing raises toxicity.","Half-life is short (about 2 hours).","Comedown includes depression and craving."),
"mdpv":("MDPV is very lipophilic and strongly blocks dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake.","Heart rate, blood pressure and temperature rise quickly.","At peak, MDPV is linked to excited delirium, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and death.","Animal studies show MDPV is more reinforcing than cocaine.","Half-life of several hours.","Psychosis can persist for days."),
"alpha-pvp":("Alpha-PVP is a potent dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor.","Heart rate and temperature rise.","At peak, excited delirium, paranoia and hyperthermia occur.","Deaths followed overheating and cardiac arrest.","Effects last a few hours.","Psychosis may last longer."),
"methylone":("Methylone releases serotonin, dopamine and noradrenaline, but less potently than MDMA.","Heart rate rises.","At peak, overheating and low sodium are the main dangers.","Redosing increases risk.","Half-life is short.","Low mood may follow."),
"eutylone":("Eutylone blocks dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake and releases some serotonin.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At peak, agitation, overheating and seizures are reported in emergency cases.","Toxicity often followed redosing.","Effects last longer than methylone.","Insomnia can last into the next night."),
"3-mmc":("3-MMC releases dopamine and noradrenaline more than serotonin.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At peak, agitation and overheating are possible.","Repeated use is linked to compulsive redosing.","Half-life is short.","Comedown brings craving."),
"other-cathinones":("These compounds vary in potency and mechanism.","Heart rate and blood pressure rise.","At peak, overheating, agitation and seizures.","Deaths have followed N-ethylpentylone and others.","Durations vary.","Psychosis may persist.")}
for k,v in X.items():
    if k in D: D[k]["ext"]={"onset":list(v[0:2]),"peak":list(v[2:4]),"comedown":list(v[4:6])}
